  "generated_text": "1. We start with the given equations:\n   \\[\n   \\log (xy^3) = 1 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\log (x^2y) = 1\n   \\]\n\n2. Using the properties of logarithms, we can rewrite these equations:\n   \\[\n   \\log (xy^3) = 1 \\implies \\log x + 3 \\log y = 1\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\log (x^2y) = 1 \\implies 2 \\log x + \\log y = 1\n   \\]\n\n3. To eliminate \\(\\log x\\) and \\(\\log y\\), we subtract the second equation from the first:\n   \\[\n   \\log x + 3 \\log y - (2 \\log x + \\log y) = 1 - 1\n   \\]\n   Simplifying this, we get:\n   \\[\n   \\log y = \\log(x) = \\frac{1}{2}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\(\\log (xy)\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}}\n\\]",
  "grade": {
